# #99 round 3: scan_for_interrogated does NOT descend into AST_LISTCOMP
# (its LISTCOMP case is an explicit no-op), so an interrogate operand that
# ONLY appears inside a listcomp body does not name-bind the outer
# variable — it stays a slot-local, and the name-keyed history query
# answers null. That null IS the pinned oracle's behavior (verified:
# `eigenscript` prints [null] here) — the oracle wins even when it looks
# odd; if a future pin changes the scan, this parity program fires.
define f() as:
    y is 1
    y is 2
    zs is [prev of y for v in [10]]
    return zs
print of (f of null)
# ...but an interrogate OUTSIDE the listcomp name-binds y for the whole
# function, so the inside query then answers too (arming interplay).
define g() as:
    y is 1
    y is 2
    zs is [prev of y for v in [10]]
    p is prev of y
    return [zs, p]
print of (g of null)
